"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/6/60/Chorley_cakes_edited.jpg/500px-Chorley_cakes_edited.jpg","w":500,"h":211,"by":"Dr Greg","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chorley_cakes_edited.jpg"}},"dish/barbecue-bologna":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/barbecue-bologna","n":"Barbecue bologna","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Southern American cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Barbecue bologna or smoked bologna is a barbecue dish from the US state of Oklahoma. It has been nicknamed Oklahoma tenderloin, Oklahoma prime rib, or Oklahoma steak. It is also sometimes served in Tennessee and Texas."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-barbecue-bologna"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbecue_bologna"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Southern American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-southern-united-states"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Bologna sausage","ingredient/bologna-sausage"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Lana's Cooking","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"lanascooking.com","note":"Lana Stuart's blog of Southern home cooking from Georgia.","url":"https://www.lanascooking.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"A Southern Soul: Southern Classics","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"asouthernsoul.com","note":"Collection of classic Southern recipes on a Southern home cooking blog.","url":"https://asouthernsoul.com/recipes/southern-classics","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Southern American cuisine","links":[["Ambrosia (fruit salad)","dish/ambrosia-fruit-salad"],["Chess pie","dish/chess-pie"],["Chicken Maryland","dish/chicken-maryland"],["Chitlins","dish/chitlins"],["Conecuh Sausage","dish/conecuh-sausage"],["Grape hull pie","dish/grape-hull-pie"],["Hash (stew)","dish/hash-stew"],["Lemon pepper wings","dish/lemon-pepper-wings"],["Mississippi mud pie","dish/mississippi-mud-pie"],["Monroe Sausage","dish/monroe-sausage"],["Moon Pie","dish/moon-pie"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/5/5b/Blue_Smoke_Bologna_Sandwich.jpg/500px-Blue_Smoke_Bologna_Sandwich.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Tom Head","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blue_Smoke_Bologna_Sandwich.jpg"}},"dish/clam-chowder":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/clam-chowder","n":"Clam chowder","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from American cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Clam chowder is any of several chowder soups in American cuisine containing clams. In addition to clams, common ingredients include diced potatoes, salt pork, and onions. It is believed that clams were used in chowder because of the relative ease of harvesting them. Clam chowder is usually served with saltine crackers or small, hexagonal oyster crackers."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-clam-chowder"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clam_chowder"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Clam","ingredient/clam"],["Salt pork","ingredient/salt-pork"]]},{"h":"More from American cuisine","links":[["Angel food cake","dish/angel-food-cake"],["Banana bread","dish/banana-bread"],["Banana pudding","dish/banana-pudding"],["Barbecue sauce","dish/barbecue-sauce"],["Beer cake","dish/beer-cake"],["Better than sex cake","dish/better-than-sex-cake"],["Blini","dish/blini"],["Blooming onion","dish/blooming-onion"],["Brown Betty (dessert)","dish/brown-betty-dessert"],["Brunswick stew","dish/brunswick-stew"],["Buffalo wing","dish/buffalo-wing"],["Butter cake","dish/butter-cake"],["Caesar salad","dish/caesar-salad"],["Caramel apple","dish/caramel-apple"],["Carbonara","dish/carbonara"],["Cheesesteak","dish/cheesesteak"],["Chicago-style hot dog","dish/chicago-style-hot-dog"],["Chicken nugget","dish/chicken-nugget"],["Chiffon cake","dish/chiffon-cake"],["Chili con carne","dish/chili-con-carne"],["Chimichanga","dish/chimichanga"],["Chow-chow (food)","dish/chow-chow-food"],["Chowder","dish/chowder"],["Cioppino","dish/cioppino"],["Clam dip","dish/clam-dip"],["Club sandwich","dish/club-sandwich"],["Cobb salad","dish/cobb-salad"],["Cobbler (food)","dish/cobbler-food"],["Cook's Corner","dish/cook-s-corner"],["Crab cake","dish/crab-cake"],["Croffle","dish/croffle"],["Dagwood Bumstead","dish/dagwood-bumstead"],["Dagwood sandwich","dish/dagwood-sandwich"],["Deep-dish pizza","dish/deep-dish-pizza"],["Desperation pie","dish/desperation-pie"],["Dirty rice","dish/dirty-rice"],["Donut hole","dish/donut-hole"],["Egg in the basket","dish/egg-in-the-basket"],["Fish and chips","dish/fish-and-chips"],["Fritter","dish/fritter"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/6/69/Quail_07_bg_041506.jpg/500px-Quail_07_bg_041506.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Jon SullivanThe original uploader was Y6y6y6 at English Wikipedia.","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Quail_07_bg_041506.jpg"}},"dish/crema-catalana":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/crema-catalana","n":"Crema catalana","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Crema catalana (Catalan for 'Catalan cream'), or crema cremada ('burnt cream'), is a Catalan dessert consisting of a custard topped with a layer of caramelized sugar. It is \"virtually identical\" to the French crème brûlée. It is made from milk, egg yolks, and sugar. Some modern recipes add cornflour. It is typically flavored with lemon zest, cinnamon, or vanilla, and a crisp caramel crust is made by caramelizing sugar placed on top of the custard."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-crema-catalana"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crema_catalana"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Andorran cuisine","cuisine/andorran-cuisine"],["Catalan cuisine","cuisine/catalan-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":6,"links":[["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Egg yolk","ingredient/egg-yolk"],["Lemon zest","ingredient/lemon-zest"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]},{"h":"More from Andorran cuisine","links":[["Arròs negre","dish/arros-negre"],["Mató","dish/mato"],["Pa de pagès","dish/pa-de-pages"],["Samfaina","dish/samfaina"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/4/49/Food_in_Catalonia_06.jpg/500px-Food_in_Catalonia_06.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Kritzolina","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Food_in_Catalonia_06.jpg"}},"ingredient/ayran":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/ayran","n":"Ayran","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Ayran ( eye-RAHN, also ayryan) is a cold savory yogurt-based beverage found throughout Central Asia, the Balkans, and the Middle East. The drink is a heterogeneous mixture that has been consumed by Turkic peoples for approximately 1,000 years. The principal ingredients are yogurt, water and salt. Herbs such as mint may be added, and some varieties are carbonated. It is a national drink of Turkey and is served in most restaurants in Bulgaria."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-ayran"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ayran"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Azerbaijani cuisine","cuisine/azerbaijani-cuisine",[["Doghramaj","dish/doghramaj"]]]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/8/8e/Fresh_ayran.jpg","w":500,"h":674,"by":"Mavigogun","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fresh_ayran.jpg"}},"ingredient/cucumis-melo":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/cucumis-melo","n":"Cucumis melo","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Cucumis melo, also known as melon or true melon, is a species of Cucumis that has been developed into many cultivated varieties. The fruit is a pepo. The flesh is either sweet or bland, with or without an aroma, and the rind can be smooth (such as honeydew), ribbed (such as European cantaloupe), wrinkled (such as Casaba melon) or netted (such as American cantaloupe). The species is sometimes referred to as muskmelon. However, there is no consensus about the usage of this term, as it can also be used as a specific name for the musky netted-rind American cantaloupe, or as a generic name for any sweet-flesh variety such the inodorous smooth-rind honeydew melon."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-cucumis-melo"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cucumis_melo"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Es buah","dish/es-buah"],["Es goyobod","dish/es-goyobod"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Honeydew (melon)","ingredient/honeydew-melon"],["Pachyrhizus erosus","ingredient/pachyrhizus-erosus"],["Pandan","ingredient/pandan"],["Papaya","ingredient/papaya"],["Pineapple","ingredient/pineapple"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Basil","ingredient/basil"],["Ice","ingredient/ice"],["Rice flour","ingredient/rice-flour"],["Syrup","ingredient/syrup"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/f/ff/Muskmelon.jpg/500px-Muskmelon.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Seth Vidal","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muskmelon.jpg"}},"dish/horumonyaki":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/horumonyaki","n":"Horumonyaki","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Japanese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Horumonyaki is a kind of Japanese cuisine made from beef or pork offal. Kitazato Shigeo, the chef of a yōshoku restaurant (one that specializes in Western-derived cuisine) in Osaka, devised this dish and registered a trademark in 1940. It was originally derived from yakiniku. The name horumon is derived from the word \"hormone\", with the intended meaning of \"stimulation\", as in the original Greek. Horumonyaki has a reputation for being a \"stamina-building\" food."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-horumonyaki"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horumonyaki"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Offal","ingredient/offal"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Just One Cookbook","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"justonecookbook.com","note":"Namiko Chen's long-running blog and videos on Japanese home cooking.","url":"https://justonecookbook.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"RecipeTin Japan","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"japan.recipetineats.com","note":"Japanese home recipes from Yumiko, a Japan-born cook, sister site to RecipeTin Eats.","url":"https://japan.recipetineats.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Japanese cuisine","links":[["Bergh–Stoutenburgh House","dish/bergh-stoutenburgh-house"],["Chikuwa","dish/chikuwa"],["Curry bread","dish/curry-bread"],["Donburi","dish/donburi"],["Fukamushicha","dish/fukamushicha"],["Gyokuro","dish/gyokuro"],["Hitsumabushi","dish/hitsumabushi"],["Iekei ramen","dish/iekei-ramen"],["Inago no tsukudani","dish/inago-no-tsukudani"],["Itamae","dish/itamae"],["Izakaya","dish/izakaya"],["Japanese cheesecake","dish/japanese-cheesecake"],["Japanese noodles","dish/japanese-noodles"],["Japanese restaurant","dish/japanese-restaurant"],["Japanese tea","dish/japanese-tea"],["Jingisukan","dish/jingisukan"],["Kaki furai","dish/kaki-furai"],["Kamaboko","dish/kamaboko"],["Kōji (food)","dish/koji-food"],["Kusa mochi","dish/kusa-mochi"],["Kushikatsu","dish/kushikatsu"],["List of Japanese restaurants","dish/list-of-japanese-restaurants"],["Makizushi","dish/makizushi"],["Miso","dish/miso"],["Miso soup","dish/miso-soup"],["Monjayaki","dish/monjayaki"],["Nikujaga","dish/nikujaga"],["Nimono","dish/nimono"],["Oden","dish/oden"],["Onigiri","dish/onigiri"],["Osechi","dish/osechi"],["Oyakodon","dish/oyakodon"],["Rainbow roll","dish/rainbow-roll"],["Ramen","dish/ramen"],["Ryōtei","dish/ryotei"],["Sashimi","dish/sashimi"],["Sencha","dish/sencha"],["Shizuoka oden","dish/shizuoka-oden"],["Sumiso","dish/sumiso"],["Sushi","dish/sushi"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/4/41/Horumonyaki.jpg/500px-Horumonyaki.jpg","w":500,"h":281,"by":"Anzai Keisuke","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Horumonyaki.jpg"}},"dish/makchang":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/makchang","n":"Makchang","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Korean cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Makchang or so-makchang (소막창; \"beef last viscus\") is a Korean dish of either the abomasum (the fourth and final stomach compartment in ruminants) of cattle or the gui (grilled dish) made of beef abomasum. The latter is also called makchang-gui (막창구이; lit. 'grilled last viscus') or so-makchang-gui (소막창구이; lit. 'grilled beef last viscus'). Dwaeji-makchang (돼지막창; lit. 'pork last viscus') means either the rectum of pig or the gui made of pork rectum, and the grilled dish is also referred to as dwaeji-makchang-gui (돼지막창구이; lit. 'grilled pork last viscus')."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-makchang"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Makchang"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Abomasum","ingredient/abomasum"],["Rectum","ingredient/rectum"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Maangchi","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"maangchi.com","note":"Maangchi's Korean recipe site and YouTube channel, one of the best known English sources for Korean cooking.","url":"https://www.maangchi.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"},{"name":"Korean Bapsang","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"koreanbapsang.com","note":"Home-style Korean recipes from Hyosun, a Korean-American mom.","url":"https://www.koreanbapsang.com/category/recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Korean cuisine","links":[["Bap (rice dish)","dish/bap-rice-dish"],["Bibim-guksu","dish/bibim-guksu"],["Bibimbap","dish/bibimbap"],["Bosintang","dish/bosintang"],["Cheonggukjang","dish/cheonggukjang"],["Doenjang","dish/doenjang"],["Garak-guksu","dish/garak-guksu"],["Gochujang","dish/gochujang"],["Gopchang","dish/gopchang"],["Hwajeon","dish/hwajeon"],["Jijimi","dish/jijimi"],["Jokbal","dish/jokbal"],["Korean restaurant","dish/korean-restaurant"],["Maekjeok","dish/maekjeok"],["Maeun-tang","dish/maeun-tang"],["Mandu-guk","dish/mandu-guk"],["Mandu-gwa","dish/mandu-gwa"],["Meat jun","dish/meat-jun"],["Meju","dish/meju"],["Morkovcha","dish/morkovcha"],["Scorched rice","dish/scorched-rice"],["Seon (food)","dish/seon-food"],["Sungnyung","dish/sungnyung"],["Tteokbokki","dish/tteokbokki"],["Wakame","dish/wakame"],["Yakgwa","dish/yakgwa"],["Yukpo","dish/yukpo"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/9/94/2%EB%AF%B8-%EB%A7%89%EC%B0%BD%EA%B5%AC%EC%9D%B4.jpg/500px-2%EB%AF%B8-%EB%A7%89%EC%B0%BD%EA%B5%AC%EC%9D%B4.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"대구광역시","lic":"CC BY 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2%EB%AF%B8-%EB%A7%89%EC%B0%BD%EA%B5%AC%EC%9D%B4.jpg"}},"dish/manchester-tart":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/manchester-tart","n":"Manchester tart","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from English cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Manchester tart is a traditional English baked tart consisting of a shortcrust pastry shell spread with raspberry jam, covered with a custard filling and topped with flakes of coconut and a maraschino cherry. A common variation has a layer of thinly sliced bananas under the custard."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-manchester-tart"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manchester_tart"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["English cuisine","cuisine/english-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":5,"links":[["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Custard","ingredient/custard"],["Fruit preserves","ingredient/fruit-preserves"],["Maraschino cherry","ingredient/maraschino-cherry"],["Shortcrust pastry","ingredient/shortcrust-pastry"]]},{"h":"More from English cuisine","links":[["Angels on horseback","dish/angels-on-horseback"],["Cabinet pudding","dish/cabinet-pudding"],["Clotted cream","dish/clotted-cream"],["Crème anglaise","dish/creme-anglaise"],["Fish and chips","dish/fish-and-chips"],["Macaroni and cheese","dish/macaroni-and-cheese"],["Maids of honour tart","dish/maids-of-honour-tart"],["Mock turtle soup","dish/mock-turtle-soup"],["Pie and mash","dish/pie-and-mash"],["Pork pie","dish/pork-pie"],["Pottage","dish/pottage"],["Windsor soup","dish/windsor-soup"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/9/93/Manchester_tart.jpg/500px-Manchester_tart.jpg","w":500,"h":512,"by":"Jamesjones79","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manchester_tart.jpg"}},"dish/miang-kham":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/miang-kham","n":"Miang kham","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Thai cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Miang kham is a traditional Southeast Asian snack from Thailand and Laos. It was introduced to the Siamese court of King Rama V by Princess Dara Rasmi. In Laos, most people call it miang. The name miang kham translates to 'one bite wrap', from miang ('food wrapped in leaves') and kham ('a bite')."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-miang-kham"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miang_kham"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Piper sarmentosum","ingredient/piper-sarmentosum"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Hot Thai Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"hot-thai-kitchen.com","note":"Pailin Chongchitnant's recipes and videos, with background on technique and ingredients.","url":"https://hot-thai-kitchen.com","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"},{"name":"SheSimmers","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"shesimmers.com","note":"Bangkok-born writer Leela Punyaratabandhu's blog on Thai home cooking.","url":"https://shesimmers.com/who-is-shesimmers","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Thai cuisine","links":[["Akhuni","dish/akhuni"],["American fried rice","dish/american-fried-rice"],["Boat noodles","dish/boat-noodles"],["Drunken noodles","dish/drunken-noodles"],["Evil jungle prince","dish/evil-jungle-prince"],["Green curry","dish/green-curry"],["Hainanese chicken rice","dish/hainanese-chicken-rice"],["Khanom babin","dish/khanom-babin"],["Khanom chin","dish/khanom-chin"],["Khanom thuai","dish/khanom-thuai"],["Khao soi","dish/khao-soi"],["La tiang","dish/la-tiang"],["Mi krop","dish/mi-krop"],["Nam ngiao","dish/nam-ngiao"],["Nam tok (food)","dish/nam-tok-food"],["Pad see ew","dish/pad-see-ew"],["Pad thai","dish/pad-thai"],["Rad naa","dish/rad-naa"],["Rice noodle roll","dish/rice-noodle-roll"],["Thai curry","dish/thai-curry"],["Thai fried rice","dish/thai-fried-rice"],["Thali","dish/thali"],["Thua nao","dish/thua-nao"],["Tom kha kai","dish/tom-kha-kai"],["Tom yum","dish/tom-yum"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/6/66/Miang_kam_ready.JPG/500px-Miang_kam_ready.JPG","w":500,"h":699,"by":"Xufanc","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miang_kam_ready.JPG"}},"ingredient/panela":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/panela","n":"Panela","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Panela or rapadura (Portuguese pronunciation: [ʁapaˈduɾɐ]) is an unrefined whole cane sugar, typical of Latin America. It is a solid form of sucrose derived from the boiling and evaporation of sugar-cane juice. Panela is known by other names in Latin America, such as chancaca in Chile, Bolivia, and Peru, piloncillo in Mexico (where panela refers to a type of cheese, queso panela). Just like brown sugar, two varieties of piloncillo are available; one is lighter (blanco) and one darker (oscuro). Unrefined, it is made from crushed sugar cane: the juice is collected, boiled, and poured into molds, where it hardens into blocks. It is a form of non-centrifugal cane sugar (NCS). Other traditional NCS include jaggery, which is used in South Asia."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-panela"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Panela"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Peruvian cuisine","cuisine/peruvian-cuisine",[["Picarones","dish/picarones"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Chancaca","ingredient/chancaca"],["Cucurbita maxima","ingredient/cucurbita-maxima"],["Fig","ingredient/fig"],["Squash (plant)","ingredient/squash-plant"],["Sweet potato","ingredient/sweet-potato"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/e/e5/Panela.JPG","w":500,"h":453,"by":"Camilo Sanchez at en.wikipedia","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panela.JPG"}},"dish/pukjai":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/pukjai","n":"Pukjai","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Koryo-saram cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Pukjai or bukjai (Koryo-mar: 북자이; букжай; [puk̚tɕ͈aɪ]) is a soybean stew dish in Koryo-saram cuisine. It is a descendant of the Korean dish doenjang-jjigae. The dish uses soybean paste (jai; тяй; 자이) as the base flavoring for the broth. Various ingredients are then added to the base stew."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-pukjai"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pukjai"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Koryo-saram cuisine","cuisine/koryo-saram-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Doenjang","ingredient/doenjang"],["Fermented bean paste","ingredient/fermented-bean-paste"],["Fermented soybean paste","ingredient/fermented-soybean-paste"]]},{"h":"More from Koryo-saram cuisine","links":[["Funchoza","dish/funchoza"],["Siryak-tyamuri","dish/siryak-tyamuri"]]}]},"dish/red-braised-pork-belly":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/red-braised-pork-belly","n":"Red braised pork belly","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Chinese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Red braised pork belly, or hong shao rou, is a classic pork dish from China, red-cooked using pork belly and a combination of ginger, garlic, aromatic spices, chilis, sugar, star anise, light and dark soy sauce, and rice wine. The dish has a melt-in-the-mouth texture that is formed as a result of a long braising process, during which the liquid reduces and becomes thick. It is generally served with steamed rice and dark green vegetables, often over holidays. The dish is often prepared with hard-boiled chicken eggs or vegetables, which are used to soak up the juices from the recipe."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-red-braised-pork-belly"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_braised_pork_belly"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"The Woks of Life","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"thewoksoflife.com","note":"Chinese and Chinese American recipes from the Leung family, running since 2013.","url":"https://thewoksoflife.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Chinese Cooking Demystified","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"youtube.com","note":"Technique-heavy YouTube channel on regional Chinese home and restaurant cooking.","url":"https://www.youtube.com/@ChineseCookingDemystified","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"},{"name":"Red House Spice","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"redhousespice.com","note":"Classic Chinese recipes from Wei Guo, who grew up in Gansu, blogging since 2017.","url":"https://redhousespice.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Chinese cuisine","links":[["Almond biscuit","dish/almond-biscuit"],["Banana roll","dish/banana-roll"],["Baozi","dish/baozi"],["Bear paw","dish/bear-paw"],["Black sesame roll","dish/black-sesame-roll"],["Century egg","dish/century-egg"],["Cha siu bao","dish/cha-siu-bao"],["Chinese restaurant","dish/chinese-restaurant"],["Dandan noodles","dish/dandan-noodles"],["Dapanji","dish/dapanji"],["Dong'an chicken","dish/dong-an-chicken"],["Dongpo pork","dish/dongpo-pork"],["Donkey burger","dish/donkey-burger"],["Doujiang","dish/doujiang"],["Drunken shrimp","dish/drunken-shrimp"],["Edible bird's nest","dish/edible-bird-s-nest"],["Fuqi feipian","dish/fuqi-feipian"],["Gaifan","dish/gaifan"],["Goubuli","dish/goubuli"],["Hainanese chicken rice","dish/hainanese-chicken-rice"],["Hog maw","dish/hog-maw"],["Hong dou tang","dish/hong-dou-tang"],["Hot dry noodles","dish/hot-dry-noodles"],["Hot pot","dish/hot-pot"],["Huangqiao sesame cake","dish/huangqiao-sesame-cake"],["Imitation shark fin soup","dish/imitation-shark-fin-soup"],["Jiaozi","dish/jiaozi"],["Jiuqu","dish/jiuqu"],["Kung Pao chicken","dish/kung-pao-chicken"],["Lanzhou beef noodles","dish/lanzhou-beef-noodles"],["Liangfen","dish/liangfen"],["Lo mai chi","dish/lo-mai-chi"],["Mao xue wang","dish/mao-xue-wang"],["Mapo tofu","dish/mapo-tofu"],["Mianwo","dish/mianwo"],["Miso soup","dish/miso-soup"],["Orange cuttlefish","dish/orange-cuttlefish"],["Osmanthus cake","dish/osmanthus-cake"],["Pickled egg","dish/pickled-egg"],["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/4/4e/Red_braised_pork_%2820141106191221%29.JPG/500px-Red_braised_pork_%2820141106191221%29.JPG","w":500,"h":373,"by":"N509FZ","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_braised_pork_(20141106191221).JPG"}}}